/bin/login from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for login, so this is the disassembly, and the names in
login.ctl come from reading the code against the structure every 1980s
login has: main takes a name and calls login(), which accounts for the
session, fixes the terminal, and execs the shell.

    disas -f login.ctl login.dist

login.dist is the binary off inode 45 of disks/dist/1011-8_dist_1.IMD,
23023 bytes, dated Aug 1982, stripped.  It is a Whitesmiths a.out: magic
9994, text at file offset 16 based at 0100 running to 557a, data based at
6000 running to 6565, no bss, and no relocations left to apply.

login.c is that disassembly read back into C - login's own functions, in
address order, ending where the library it was linked against begins.
It is K&R throughout and is compiled here with the tree's ccc.  It is a
reconstruction of behaviour and not the original source, and it does not
rebuild this binary.  See the caveats.

It is written against THIS tree's library and not the one the binary was
linked with.  login was built with Whitesmiths C, whose library - libwsc.a
and libu.a on the C system disk - this tree does not have, so the calls
are made by the names the tree uses now: lenstr is strlen, cmpstr is
strcmp, cpystr is strcpy followed by strcat, and instr is strchr.  The
comments in login.c still record which of the 1982 entries each call site
was.  The rest the binary called - gets, malloc, exit, perror, ctime,
execv, the syscall wrappers - are libc and libu here under the same names,
with ctime() taking one argument instead of two.


the Whitesmiths runtime
-----------------------

The calling convention is not a guess.  The compiler's own library is on
the V2-1 disk as LIBC.80, and it carries the runtime as two members whose
code is byte-for-byte what login links at 53c1:

                        ent.o, codelen 11

    c.ent   21 00 00 39 eb e3 e9   ld hl,0; add hl,sp; ex de,hl; ex (sp),hl; jp (hl)
    c.ihl   e9                     jp (hl)          - indirect call
    c.ret   eb f9 d1 c9            ex de,hl; ld sp,hl; pop de; ret

                        ents.o, codelen 41

    c.ents  c1 21 fe ff 39 eb e5   pop bc; ld hl,-2; add hl,sp; ex de,hl; push hl;
            2a<r4> e5 2a<r3> e5    save the three register cells
            2a<r2> e5 69 60 e9     ld l,c; ld h,b; jp (hl)
    c.rets  21 fa ff 19 f9 e1      ld hl,-6; add hl,de; ld sp,hl; pop hl;
            22<r2> e1 22<r3> e1    restore them
            22<r4> d1 c9           pop de; ret

There is no frame-size byte hiding in either one: the members are complete
at 11 and 41 bytes, and searching every image under disks/ for a variant
that skips an inline operand finds nothing but coincidental byte pairs.
A function's locals are allocated by compiler-emitted code placed
immediately after the prologue call, as in main here:

    015e: call c.ents
    0161: ld hl,fe00h ; add hl,sp ; ld sp,hl      frame 200

The frames in login are 200 (main), 202 (login, and the password check),
2a (acct), 204 (lastlogin) and 600 (the shell exec, which holds an argv
array).

From that, the convention.  The caller pushes arguments right to left, so
arg0 is on top, then calls.  The callee opens with call c.ent or c.ents,
which leaves DE two words below the frame bottom, so

    DE+0   scratch, where the old DE is parked
    DE+2   the return address into the caller
    DE+4   arg0
    DE+6   arg1

and a char argument is the low byte at DE+4.  Results come back in BC, or
in C for a char.  c.ret/c.rets return past the callee's own prologue call
straight to the caller, so the pair is invisible from outside.  The caller
discards the arguments with one pop af per two-byte argument - as many
pops as pushes, and no more.  Three register variables live in the cells
c.r2, c.r3 and c.r4, at 655f, 6561 and 6563 here; a function that uses the
plain c.ent prologue cannot touch them, so its register variables are put
in the data segment instead, which is why the signal loops keep their
counters at 607e, 607f and 6080.

The same convention is spelled out in the A-Natural listing ECHO.8 on the
V2-0 disk, which is the compiler's own output for echo.c: its main reads
hl=&04+de for arg0 and hl=&06+de for arg1, and its c.r2/c.r3/c.r4 are the
same three cells.


the layout
----------

0100  chdr.r, the startup: pops the argument count into BC, computes the
      argv base from the stack, pushes argv then count, calls main, and
      passes main's result to exit.
0111  login's own literals begin - /etc/utmp, /usr/spool/mail/,
      /usr/adm/lastlog, /usr/adm/wtmp, "Name: ", /etc/banner, and then the
      rest, interleaved with the code as the compiler emits them.
015e  login's own code: main, login, acct, type, chkname, nographic,
      alnumdash, lastlogin, and the password and signal machinery.
      A library routine is one that has no business in login: the
      formatted output engine is at 12d3 (_doprnt), and the stdio layer -
      fopen, getfile, openfile, finit, fclose - follows it.  The passwd
      reader that owns the /etc/passwd literal at 1151 is a library
      getpwnam, not login's own.
53c1  the runtime above, c.ent through c.rets, ending at 53f4.
557a  end of text.
6000  login's own data, up to about 6236; the library's stdio pool of
      sixteen 16-byte FILE slots starts at 6237.
6565  end of data.  Nothing is in bss.


the library above it
--------------------

login's own text ends well below where an earlier reading of this put it,
and everything above the seam is the C library that was statically bound
into the binary.  The addresses, and the names read off their behaviour:

    12d3  _doprnt                190d  fopen        1a29  getfile
    1a9b  openfile               1b64  finit        1bd6  fclose
    1e7b  fseek                  1ff4  rewind       2062  gets
    2118  fgets                  2220  strlen       2258  _filbuf
    2411  _flsbuf                256e  _findbuf     2681  fclose's
                                                                buffer
                                                                release
    2693  calloc                 2712  free-list    2739  size
                                        head            conversion
    275d  malloc                 2869  free         29c2  exit
    2b14  ctime                  35e7  ttyname      3719  ttyslot
    3a0f  streq                  3a5a  strcpy       3b8e  contains
    3e59  strlen                 40c0  perror       4274  execv
    42a7  lseek                  4629  setuid       4e05  16x16
                                                                multiply
    50f9  32-bit multiply        53c1  the runtime above

and between 3e59 and 4e05 the system-call wrappers the calls above name -
chdir, chmod, chown, close, creat, exec, exit, fork, fstat, getpid,
getuid, gtty, kill, link, mknod, mount, nice, open, pause, pipe, read,
seek, signal, _signal, sleep, stat, stime, stty, sync, time, umount,
unlink, wait, write.

The names are argued, not read: the binary is stripped, so each is what
the routine does.  Corroboration comes from the C system disk
(disks/dist/1014-8_c_1.IMD), whose /lib holds the same library by member
name - libwsc.a carries lenstr.o, cmpstr.o, cpystr.o, instr.o, crypt.o,
alloc.o, exit.o, ckfd.o and ent.o/ents.o (the runtime itself), and libu.a
is exactly the system-call wrappers listed above.  So the library's own
names for four routines login calls are lenstr, cmpstr, cpystr and instr,
which is what the behaviour-derived names streq/strcpy/contains/strlen
above really are.

What is NOT there is worth as much: getpwnam, endpwent, ttyname and
ttyslot appear in no archive on that disk and in no library this tree
builds.  That is why the passwd module in the upper middle of login's
text is read as login's own rather than as a library call.

The library's storage is the other half of the data segment.  The stdio
FILE is sixteen bytes and the pool of sixteen of them starts at 6237, so
stdin is &_iob[0] and its _flag byte is at 623d; the allocator keeps an
address-ordered circular free list in four-byte units with a four-byte
header in front of every block.  login's own globals stop at about 6236.


caveats
-------

This is a reconstruction of behaviour, not byte-identical source.  The
original was written for the compiler that made the binary, and the tree's
ccc is a different compiler with a different calling convention, so
login.c says what the disassembly does and does not produce these bytes.

It builds.  ccc -c takes it clean against this tree's headers with no ws
name left in it, and ccc -o links it against libc.a and libu.a with no
undefined symbols - text 10265, data 2252, bss 0.

That took three routines the tree does not have, which are written at the
end of login.c rather than called from a library.  In 1982 all three were
members of something: getpwent.o for the reader, and ttyname/ttyslot for
the /dev scan.  No library this tree builds carries any of them, and
owner.c and ps.c report the same gap and answer it the same way, so
these follow the tree and not the archive:

    getpwnam, endpwent   the /etc/passwd reader at 1103 and 119b,
                         with getpwent at 0f0b and fld at 1035
    ttyname, ttyslot     the /dev directory scan at 35e7 and 3719
    crypt                the password hash at 0c37

crypt is the one that was login's own all along and not a library call:
its seed "Here is is !?!" is in login's literal pool, and it is the
pre-DES big-multiply hash and not the DES one whose object is in libwsc.a.

The library boundary in the middle of the text remains the weakest part
of the map.  The linker's order is what settles it - chdr.r first, then
login.r, then the library members - so login's own text is the low region,
but where exactly it stops among the string and system-call helpers in the
middle is argued from what each routine does rather than from a symbol
table, because the binary is stripped.  The passwd module at 0f08-11b8 was
matched byte for byte against getpwent.o in libS.a on the C system disk,
which is the one place a symbol table did settle it; that match also
confirms the record layout, because the module's own DB directives at
61cc and 61cd say uid and gid are one byte each, exactly as <pwd.h> here
has them.

Two things are still not wired: the makefile and GNUmakefile beside this
README are not in the tree, so login is not in cmd/DIRS and nothing in
the build reaches this directory.  And nothing here has been run - the
reconstruction is read, not exercised, and crypt in particular is a
reconstruction of an algorithm whose output nothing has checked.
